WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:09.031
The life of Levi Strauss is a powerful example of the American dream.

2
00:00:10.152 --> 00:00:15.694
His chances for success in his native Germany were limited, but he immigrated to the United States as

3
00:00:15.754 --> 00:00:18.415
a young man and became a prominent businessman.

4
00:00:19.596 --> 00:00:25.618
A century after his death, he is famous as the inventor of blue jeans, which are worn worldwide.

5
00:00:26.038 --> 00:00:27.699
He could not have done it alone, however.

6
00:00:28.819 --> 00:00:34.221
His commitment to family, community, and hard work enabled Levi Strauss to succeed.

7
00:00:35.421 --> 00:00:37.782
Levi was not always called Levi.

8
00:00:38.102 --> 00:00:44.444
He was born Loeb Strauss in Buttenheim, Germany, on February 26, 1829.

9
00:00:45.504 --> 00:00:48.465
He had three older brothers and three older sisters.

10
00:00:48.845 --> 00:00:52.426
His father, Hirsch, worked hard to support the large family.

11
00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:58.408
But Hirsch was Jewish, and in nineteenth-century Germany, his choices were restricted.

12
00:00:59.732 --> 00:01:05.317
The government legislated where Jewish people could live, what jobs they could do, and even how many could

13
00:01:05.357 --> 00:01:06.257
marry each year.

14
00:01:07.358 --> 00:01:11.321
Hirsch worked in an approved job; he was a peddler of dry goods.

15
00:01:12.562 --> 00:01:20.168
The Strauss family might have stayed in Germany forever, but in 1845 Hirsch died from tuberculosis, and everything

16
00:01:20.228 --> 00:01:24.812
changed. First, the two oldest brothers immigrated to New York.

17
00:01:25.352 --> 00:01:29.974
In 1847 Levi also left Germany with his mother and two of his sisters.

18
00:01:31.034 --> 00:01:36.757
They began the difficult journey to America with very few things from their past; they couldn't take much

19
00:01:36.837 --> 00:01:39.198
luggage in steerage class, where the poor traveled.

20
00:01:40.258 --> 00:01:44.760
After a ten-day coach ride to a port, they sailed from Germany to New York City.

21
00:01:45.841 --> 00:01:52.383
The family spent six weeks below deck, cramped among many other passengers—with no fresh air, little food or

22
00:01:52.423 --> 00:01:54.004
water, and no toilets.

23
00:01:55.296 --> 00:01:59.117
Levi Strauss was eighteen years old when he finally saw America.

24
00:02:00.178 --> 00:02:05.699
His older brothers had already started a dry-goods business there, and Strauss began to peddle around the New

25
00:02:05.739 --> 00:02:06.459
York area.

26
00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:12.541
He lugged around a hundred pounds of goods and came home once a week to restock and see

27
00:02:12.561 --> 00:02:13.081
his family.

28
00:02:14.202 --> 00:02:17.043
Strauss was a good salesman, and the business prospered.

29
00:02:17.363 --> 00:02:21.484
He changed his first name to Levi and taught himself to speak English.

30
00:02:22.696 --> 00:02:26.479
In January 1853 he became an American citizen.

31
00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:32.344
With a new name and a new country, Levi Strauss was ready to take on a new adventure.

32
00:02:33.505 --> 00:02:39.610
Gold had been discovered in California in 1848, and thousands of people moved there to find their fortunes.

33
00:02:40.811 --> 00:02:46.595
Strauss was sure that the miners would need supplies, so he decided to move to San Francisco and

34
00:02:46.676 --> 00:02:47.636
sell his goods there.

35
00:02:48.737 --> 00:02:51.920
However, the trip from New York to California was not easy.

36
00:02:53.212 --> 00:02:57.015
Strauss chose the fastest route, across the Isthmus of Panama.

37
00:02:58.095 --> 00:03:04.180
He left New York in February 1853 on a steamship that took him down America's east coast to

38
00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:11.344
Panama. He rode on the Panama Railway until the train tracks stopped, and then he traveled upriver in

39
00:03:11.384 --> 00:03:11.825
a canoe.

40
00:03:12.965 --> 00:03:18.829
After that he and his supplies transferred onto mules for the final twenty miles to the Pacific Ocean.

41
00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.472
The harsh climate made the journey even more difficult.

42
00:03:23.452 --> 00:03:29.414
Strauss endured sweltering heat and torrential rains and spent many nights in bug-infested tents.

43
00:03:30.635 --> 00:03:33.956
He must have been thrilled to finally board the ship to San Francisco.

44
00:03:35.097 --> 00:03:39.779
There are many legends describing how Strauss went from selling dry goods to making pants.

45
00:03:41.359 --> 00:03:46.841
One is that when his ship arrived in San Francisco, people rowed out and bought nearly everything he

46
00:03:46.901 --> 00:03:48.962
had brought before he even came ashore.

47
00:03:50.043 --> 00:03:55.537
When a customer told him, "You should have brought pants," Strauss made pants with the only thing he

48
00:03:55.598 --> 00:03:57.038
had left, canvas.

49
00:03:58.279 --> 00:04:03.902
Another story is that several weeks after his arrival, a miner complained to Strauss that the pockets of

50
00:04:03.942 --> 00:04:08.085
his pants wore out too quickly, and he lost his gold through the holes.

51
00:04:08.545 --> 00:04:11.467
So Strauss sewed sturdy canvas pants for him.

52
00:04:12.568 --> 00:04:15.870
The truth is that Strauss did not begin by selling pants.

53
00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:23.073
He opened a dry-goods store in San Francisco and traveled through gold miners' camps peddling his wares.

54
00:04:24.233 --> 00:04:26.294
His family helped in this new undertaking.

55
00:04:27.354 --> 00:04:33.296
His brothers in New York shipped the goods out west, and Strauss' brother-in-law moved to San Francisco to

56
00:04:33.336 --> 00:04:34.256
help run the store.

57
00:04:35.536 --> 00:04:43.799
Strauss had been correct thinking miners would be eager customers; between 1853 and 1866, business increased so much

58
00:04:44.079 --> 00:04:45.579
that Levi Strauss Co.

59
00:04:45.839 --> 00:04:48.460
had to move to bigger locations four times.

60
00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:54.002
But a tailor named Jacob Davis had an idea that led to even greater success.

61
00:04:55.142 --> 00:04:58.964
Davis bought durable fabric from Strauss and used it to make pants.

62
00:05:00.205 --> 00:05:05.207
The tailor found that the seams of the pockets ripped, causing miners to lose the gold they were

63
00:05:05.247 --> 00:05:09.048
carrying. So he put small metal rivets at these weaker spots.

64
00:05:10.269 --> 00:05:15.291
His customers loved the new pants so much that Davis could not make enough of them by himself.

65
00:05:16.471 --> 00:05:21.505
Davis wrote to Strauss, suggesting that they take out a patent on the riveted pants together.

66
00:05:22.665 --> 00:05:29.867
Strauss agreed, and he and Davis received a patent for these "waist overalls" on May 20, 1873, and

67
00:05:29.928 --> 00:05:32.048
soon opened a factory to manufacture them.

68
00:05:33.288 --> 00:05:37.190
The denim waist overalls were intended to be worn over miners' clothes.

69
00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:43.792
They were sewn with bright orange thread to match the copper rivets, and the pockets were embellished with

70
00:05:43.812 --> 00:05:45.172
a unique V design.

71
00:05:46.252 --> 00:05:51.895
Within a year, Strauss and Davis had sold over twenty thousand pairs of the distinctive pants.

72
00:05:53.056 --> 00:05:54.317
Levi Strauss Co.

73
00:05:54.397 --> 00:05:57.098
became famous for the design and continued to grow.

74
00:05:57.739 --> 00:06:00.300
Soon, miners weren't the only ones wearing them.

75
00:06:01.441 --> 00:06:07.444
Others valued the practical, durable pants, and people began to wear the waist overalls instead of pants, not

76
00:06:07.545 --> 00:06:07.905
over them.

77
00:06:09.005 --> 00:06:13.148
Jeans, as we now know them, were born—and they were called "Levi's."

78
00:06:14.312 --> 00:06:20.515
As more people arrived in San Francisco, Strauss' business grew even faster, and he became a pillar of

79
00:06:20.535 --> 00:06:21.875
the San Francisco community.

80
00:06:23.136 --> 00:06:25.897
He donated to both Jewish and Christian charities.

81
00:06:27.017 --> 00:06:32.219
He took good care of his employees, and to maintain a friendly workplace, he insisted they call him

82
00:06:32.459 --> 00:06:33.780
Levi, not Mr.

83
00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:40.302
Strauss. Strauss treated the residents of San Francisco as part of his family, and they embraced him in

84
00:06:40.343 --> 00:06:45.801
return. Strauss devoted his life to his work; he never married or had kids.

85
00:06:47.022 --> 00:06:51.725
However, he treated his sister's children as if they were his own, and when they were old enough,

86
00:06:51.985 --> 00:06:53.887
he turned Levi Strauss Co.

87
00:06:54.067 --> 00:06:55.208
over to his nephews.

88
00:06:56.348 --> 00:06:57.689
But Strauss never retired.

89
00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:03.553
Each morning he donned a dark suit, put on his stovepipe hat, and walked to his office.

90
00:07:03.573 --> 00:07:09.557
"I don't believe that a man who once forms the habit of being busy can retire and be

91
00:07:09.597 --> 00:07:10.798
contented," he said.

92
00:07:11.899 --> 00:07:16.862
He worked until his death at age 73, on September 26, 1902.

93
00:07:18.202 --> 00:07:24.906
Levi Strauss started out poor in Germany, worked hard, and found his fortune in America with the world's

94
00:07:24.986 --> 00:07:27.707
most beloved article of clothing—blue jeans.

95
00:07:28.848 --> 00:07:34.671
Although "Levi's" jeans bear the name of only one man, the business succeeded because of the support Strauss

96
00:07:34.711 --> 00:07:37.312
received from family and community along the way.

97
00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:45.202
More than a century after his waist overalls first appeared, jeans are worn by nearly everyone—rich and poor,

98
00:07:45.523 --> 00:07:47.083
young and old, everywhere.

99
00:07:48.283 --> 00:07:54.346
Levi Strauss, a man who valued family as much as work, would be proud that his descendants still

100
00:07:54.406 --> 00:07:55.746
run the company he founded.
